$DIR/main.p:20:20: f was moved to r at line 19, so it can no longer be used here
    owned[File] is an owner type: its values own what they hold and release it
    when they are dropped. Assigning an owner moves it instead of copying it, so
    that what it owns has exactly one owner. Since line 19, r owns it and f
    holds nothing.
    Go copies such values freely and its garbage collector frees what they refer
    to when nothing refers to it any more. Plutonium has no garbage collector:
    an owner frees what it owns when it is dropped, so there must be exactly
    one.
    If r only needs to look at the value, give it a pointer instead:
        r := &f
    If this is the last use of f, move it before line 19, where f still holds
    its value.
    (see "Owners" in the Plutonium spec, section 6.4)
    (run "pluto explain E3301" for a longer explanation)

$DIR/main.p:33:7: cannot assert n to owned[File]: n is a Namer, which only lends the value it holds
    Converting an owner to an ordinary interface such as Namer lends it: the
    interface looks at the owner, which still belongs to the variable that was
    converted. Taking an owned[File] out of n would create a second owner.
    Go copies such values freely and its garbage collector frees what they refer
    to when nothing refers to it any more. Plutonium has no garbage collector:
    an owner frees what it owns when it is dropped, so there must be exactly
    one.
    Call the value's methods through an interface type instead:
        if s, ok := n.(fmt.Stringer); ok {
            use(s.String())
        }
    or move it into an owning interface in the first place:
        type Text owner interface{ String() string }
    (see "Owners" in the Plutonium spec, section 6.4)
    (run "pluto explain E3308" for a longer explanation)

$DIR/main.p:42:10: r may have been moved at line 39, so it cannot be used here
    Resource is an owner type: its values own what they hold and release it when
    they are dropped. r is moved out by the type assertion r.(owned[File]) at
    line 39 on some paths to this point and not on others. On those paths r
    holds nothing afterwards, and the compiler cannot let a value be used where
    it may already belong to someone else.
    Go copies such values freely and its garbage collector frees what they refer
    to when nothing refers to it any more. Plutonium has no garbage collector:
    an owner frees what it owns when it is dropped, so there must be exactly
    one.
    Use r before line 39, or give it a new value on the path that moves it:
        r = ...
    (see "Owners" in the Plutonium spec, section 6.4)
    (run "pluto explain E3302" for a longer explanation)

$DIR/main.p:27:9: cannot return n, which points into the object f refers to, which f keeps alive in lent
    n points into the object f refers to, which f keeps alive in lent. f
    (declared at main.p:25) is an owned[File]: the object it refers to lives
    only while f holds it, and is dropped when f is dropped at the end of its
    scope in lent, or released when it is the last reference. A pointer into the
    object, or an interface that lends it, is a view that may not outlive f.
    When lent returns, f is dropped and the object freed, so n would point at
    freed memory.
    Go would move this memory to the heap, and its garbage collector would free
    it later. Plutonium has no garbage collector and never moves memory to the
    heap behind the program's back, so that every allocation is visible in the
    code.
    Return f itself: an owned box moves to the caller, and a shared or synced
    one gives the caller its own reference:
        return f
    (see "Views" in the Plutonium spec, section 6.3)
    (run "pluto explain E3001" for a longer explanation)
